---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
const locale = 'en' as const;
---
<GuideLayout
  locale={locale}
  canonicalPath="/guides/how-to-choose-bakery-tray-washer/"
  guideTitle="How to Choose a Bakery Tray Washer"
  title="How to Choose a Bakery Tray Washer — A Buying Guide"
  description="A practical buying guide for choosing the right commercial bakery tray washer. Throughput, chamber size, certifications, budget, and the questions to ask vendors."
>
  <h1 class="mb-4">How to Choose a Bakery Tray Washer</h1>
  <p class="text-lg text-bakery-navy/80 mb-8">If you're shopping for a commercial bakery tray washer for the first time, this guide will walk you through the decisions that actually matter. Skip the marketing fluff and pricing tricks.</p>

  <h2 class="mb-3">Step 1: Measure your largest tray</h2>
  <p>Before anything else: get a ruler and measure the largest tray, pan, or bowl you regularly need to wash. Write down: length × width × height in millimeters.</p>
  <p class="mt-3">This single number eliminates ~70% of dishwashers from consideration. A 600×400 mm baking tray immediately rules out every American hood-type dishwasher except the JD-3 (which uses a 650×550 rack).</p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Step 2: Calculate your daily volume</h2>
  <p>Count items washed per day. Categories: trays, pans, bowls, utensils, dishware. Total daily items × 30 days × 12 months = annual cleaning volume. This determines whether you need an undercounter (under 80/day), hood-type (80–300/day), or conveyor (300+/day).</p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Step 3: Match throughput to peak load</h2>
  <p>Average daily volume isn't the constraint — peak hour is. If you have a 200-cover lunch service, your post-lunch dishwashing wave is the constraint. Calculate items-per-peak-hour. Then look at machine throughput.</p>
  <p class="mt-3">JD-3: 60 racks/hour. With 8 items per rack average, that's 480 items/hour. More than enough for any small-to-mid commercial operation.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Step 4: Verify electrical & water requirements</h2>
  <p>Most commercial hood-type dishwashers require 380V three-phase electrical. If your kitchen only has 220V single-phase, you have two options: install a 380V circuit (~$1,500 in most countries) or buy a 220V model (slower cycle, less common). Specify which you have when requesting a quote.</p>
  <p class="mt-3">Water: confirm cold-water inlet pressure (most dishwashers need 2.5–6 kg/cm²) and drainage access within 1.5 m.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Step 5: Set your budget honestly</h2>
  <p>Commercial dishwasher pricing:</p>
  <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mt-3">
    <li>Undercounter: $1,500–$4,600</li>
    <li>Hood-type budget: $4,400–$5,000 (JD-3, low-end Chinese imports)</li>
    <li>Hood-type mainstream: $5,000–$8,000 (Jackson, CMA)</li>
    <li>Hood-type premium: $7,000–$11,000 (Hobart, Winterhalter)</li>
    <li>Conveyor: $15,000+</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="mt-3">Factor in: shipping ($300–$1,500), installation ($200–$800), local taxes/duties (varies wildly by country — typically 5–30%).</p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Step 6: Verify certifications for your market</h2>
  <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mt-3">
    <li><strong>US:</strong> NSF and UL listings preferred but not strictly required for most jurisdictions</li>
    <li><strong>EU, UK, Middle East, GCC:</strong> CE certification required</li>
    <li><strong>Asia-Pacific:</strong> CE certification widely accepted</li>
    <li><strong>Latin America, Africa:</strong> CE certification widely accepted</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Step 7: Ask vendors these specific questions</h2>
  <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 mt-3">
    <li>What is the internal chamber dimension? (Not the external machine size.)</li>
    <li>What is the standard rack size?</li>
    <li>What is your warranty? (Length, parts vs labor coverage.)</li>
    <li>What is lead time from order to delivery to my location?</li>
    <li>What is the spare parts ship time to my country?</li>
    <li>Can you provide a reference customer in my country/region?</li>
    <li>What is the actual water consumption per cycle, measured?</li>
    <li>Is the booster heater calibrated to maintain &gt;82°C final rinse?</li>
    <li>What detergent and rinse aid do you recommend?</li>
    <li>Show me the spec sheet PDF.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Step 8: Get multiple quotes</h2>
  <p>Quote at least 3 manufacturers. Request the same configuration from each. Compare quoted prices, included accessories, shipping, and warranty terms. The price differences will surprise you.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Step 9: Verify the supplier exists</h2>
  <p>For Chinese imports specifically: verify the manufacturer (not just a trading company) by requesting a video tour of the factory and a copy of their ISO 9001 certificate. Reputable manufacturers respond within 24 hours.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-8 mb-3">Step 10: Plan for installation and training</h2>
  <p>Schedule your installation date BEFORE you order. Coordinate plumber, electrician, and your kitchen downtime. Most installations take 2–4 hours. Operator training (30 minutes) should happen the same day with all staff who will use the machine.</p>
</GuideLayout>
